WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.629
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.338
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.638 --> 00:00:15.147
Unit nineteen: Stylistic Fronting. Today you will create vivid narration with fronted adverbials.

4
00:00:15.447 --> 00:00:18.227
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.562
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:24.824
Down came the rain.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:29.117
Down came the rain means a fronted adverbial with inversion.

8
00:00:29.717 --> 00:00:31.043
Down came the rain.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.790
Your turn. Say: Down came the rain.

10
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:39.145
Down came the rain.

11
00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:41.198
Away she ran.

12
00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:45.077
Away she ran means a fronted adverb of direction.

13
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:46.963
Away she ran.

14
00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.051
Your turn. Say: Away she ran.

15
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.261
Away she ran.

16
00:00:55.017 --> 00:00:56.517
Adverbial fronting.

17
00:00:57.517 --> 00:01:00.433
Adverbial fronting means moving an adverbial to the front.

18
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.270
Adverbial fronting creates drama.

19
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:07.047
Your turn. Say: Adverbial fronting.

20
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.714
Adverbial fronting.

21
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.892
Literary inversion.

22
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:18.183
Literary inversion means verb before subject in writing.

23
00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:20.365
A literary inversion.

24
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.206
Your turn. Say: Literary inversion.

25
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:28.753
Literary inversion.

26
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:31.156
Descriptive emphasis.

27
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.369
Descriptive emphasis means emphasis in descriptions.

28
00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:37.600
Descriptive emphasis.

29
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:41.509
Your turn. Say: Descriptive emphasis.

30
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:46.076
Descriptive emphasis.

31
00:01:46.817 --> 00:01:48.143
Narrative style.

32
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.974
Narrative style means the style of storytelling.

33
00:01:52.583 --> 00:01:54.002
A narrative style.

34
00:01:54.617 --> 00:01:57.548
Your turn. Say: Narrative style.

35
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.819
Narrative style.

36
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:03.863
Dramatic effect.

37
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.291
Dramatic effect means an impression of drama.

38
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:09.279
A dramatic effect.

39
00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:13.012
Your turn. Say: Dramatic effect.

40
00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:17.135
Dramatic effect.

41
00:02:17.883 --> 00:02:19.047
Creative writing.

42
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.491
Creative writing means imaginative writing.

43
00:02:23.083 --> 00:02:24.270
Creative writing.

44
00:02:24.883 --> 00:02:27.594
Your turn. Say: Creative writing.

45
00:02:30.433 --> 00:02:31.678
Creative writing.

46
00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:36.176
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:37.017 --> 00:02:41.570
Visal, why would a storyteller write Down came the rain instead of The rain came down?

48
00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:52.954
It is stylistic fronting. The adverb Down moves to the front, then the verb, then the noun subject. It paints a quick, vivid picture and builds suspense.

49
00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.179
Are there rules?

50
00:02:56.283 --> 00:03:11.814
The subject must be a noun for the inversion: Away ran the thief. With a pronoun, the order does not invert: Away he ran. Intransitive verbs of movement or position work best.

51
00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:14.394
Where is it used?

52
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:27.319
In stories, speeches and descriptions. In everyday talk it is rare, so use it deliberately, one or two times in a passage, to mark a turning point.

53
00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:30.501
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:38.864
Grammar focus: Stylistic fronting: adverbial plus verb plus noun subject (or adverbial plus pronoun plus verb).

55
00:03:39.164 --> 00:03:40.490
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:42.568
Down came the rain.

57
00:03:43.483 --> 00:03:44.868
Away ran the thief.

58
00:03:45.783 --> 00:03:47.469
Away she ran into the night.

59
00:03:48.433 --> 00:03:55.381
Front an adverb of place or direction. A noun subject comes after the verb; a pronoun subject stays before it.

60
00:03:56.117 --> 00:03:59.570
Be careful! Don't say: Away ran she.

61
00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:03.219
Say: Away she ran.

62
00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:05.345
Pronouns do not invert.

63
00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:08.820
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:10.936
The Storm Breaks.

65
00:04:11.236 --> 00:04:14.362
Dark clouds filled the sky, and then down came the rain.

66
00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.459
Out of the shadows stepped a stranger.

67
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:21.035
Away ran the children, laughing and shouting.

68
00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:24.370
Up the hill she climbed without looking back.

69
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:28.028
The fronted adverbials make the story feel alive.

70
00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:33.065
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:33.917 --> 00:04:35.150
Down blank the rain.

72
00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:38.384
A, came. B, come. C, coming.

73
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:43.921
The answer is A: came.

74
00:04:45.083 --> 00:04:46.572
Away blank the thief.

75
00:04:46.872 --> 00:04:49.945
A, ran. B, run. C, running.

76
00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.685
The answer is A: ran.

77
00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.559
Which is correct with a pronoun?

78
00:04:58.859 --> 00:05:03.395
A, Away ran she.. B, Away she ran.. C, She away ran..

79
00:05:06.933 --> 00:05:09.490
The answer is B: Away she ran..

80
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.963
Stylistic fronting is common in blank.

81
00:05:13.263 --> 00:05:16.928
A, stories. B, recipes. C, menus.

82
00:05:20.467 --> 00:05:22.420
The answer is A: stories.

83
00:05:23.583 --> 00:05:26.663
According to the reading, who stepped out of the shadows?

84
00:05:26.963 --> 00:05:31.336
A, a stranger. B, a teacher. C, a child.

85
00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.225
The answer is A: a stranger.

86
00:05:38.433 --> 00:05:41.805
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:45.718
Write a short, dramatic sentence using stylistic fronting.

88
00:05:54.517 --> 00:05:55.947
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:56.247 --> 00:06:05.624
Out of the darkness leapt a huge dog. Away ran the frightened children. I used fronting to make the scene fast and dramatic, like in a short story.

90
00:06:05.924 --> 00:06:07.715
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:08.617 --> 00:06:17.875
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and stylistic fronting: adverbial plus verb plus noun subject (or adverbial plus pronoun plus verb).

92
00:06:18.175 --> 00:06:23.289
Today's value is appreciation of beauty. Learn to notice and value the beauty around you.

93
00:06:23.589 --> 00:06:26.020
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:29.135
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

